$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The underlying claim was a supplier's claim for unpaid invoices for stainless steel utensils supplied to the respondent buyer. The buyer's defence was quality: it said the goods were defective, had been rejected by its own customer — Walmart USA — and had necessitated a settlement payment of approximately Rs. 2.29 crores at its end. The supplier invoked the MSMED machinery; conciliation before the Facilitation Council failed; and the Council referred the dispute to the DIAC under Section 18(3), where a sole arbitrator made an award. The buyer then petitioned under Section 34 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996.</p>

<h2>The structural objection and its rejection</h2>
<p>The petition's lead argument did not attack the merits so much as the machinery: the arbitrator, it was said, lacked authority to render an independent award because his mandate originated in a Council reference rather than a consensual arbitration agreement. Justice Harish Vaidyanathan Shankar rejected the premise:</p>
<div class="note">
<p>"Once such a statutory reference is made by the Council to an arbitral institution under Section 18(3) of the MSMED Act, the arbitral proceedings necessarily proceed independently in accordance with the provisions" of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996.</p>
</div>
<p>The fiction enacted by Section 18(3) — that the A&amp;C Act applies as if there were an arbitration agreement between the parties — is complete. The institution administers, the arbitrator adjudicates, and the award carries the same force as any other domestic award. The Delhi MSEFC Rules, which contemplate exactly this institutional referral, reinforce the design.</p>

<h2>The MSMED dispute pipeline in Delhi</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Reference.</strong> The supplier files its delayed-payment claim (typically through the MSME Samadhaan portal) before the Delhi MSEFC under Section 18(1).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Conciliation.</strong> The Council conducts or refers conciliation under Section 18(2). Settlement ends the matter; failure triggers the next stage.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Arbitration.</strong> Under Section 18(3), the Council arbitrates itself or refers to an institution — in Delhi, ordinarily the DIAC — and the A&amp;C Act governs in full.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Award and challenge.</strong> The award is enforceable as a decree; a buyer's challenge under Section 34 requires the 75% deposit mandated by Section 19 MSMED Act.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the decision means for suppliers and buyers</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>For MSME suppliers.</strong> The institutional route is robust: an award obtained through a DIAC reference will not be undone by structural objections to the arbitrator's mandate. Registration, documentation of supplies, and invoice discipline remain the real battlegrounds.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>For buyers.</strong> Defences on quality and set-off must be fought fully in the arbitration itself — with evidence of rejection, debit notes and correspondence — because the Section 34 court will not re-weigh them, and the Section 19 deposit makes a speculative challenge expensive.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>For both.</strong> The MSMED overlay changes remedies and economics, not the adjudicative standard: the arbitration is a real arbitration, and its award is as durable as the record built before the arbitrator.</div>
</div>
